<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dartmoor</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dartmoor is an upland area of granite moorland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, protected as a national park since 1951. It holds one of the densest concentrations of prehistoric remains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, including stone rows, hut circles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> field boundaries known as reaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">Tin</a> was worked in its streams for centuries, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its tinners were governed by their own stannary courts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dartmoor.jpg" alt="Dartmoor" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Myself - Herby talk thyme · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_to_Sharpitor_from_Meavy.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
